Fix cross-compile: bindgen's compile-time struct-layout assertions
(size_of::<T>() - N, e.g. FILE 216, tm 56) come out sized for whatever
target `--clang_arg(--target=...)` was given. This build.rs reads
Cargo's `HOST` env var, which is the triple of the machine *running*
the build (e.g. x86_64-...), not `TARGET` (the triple actually being
compiled for). On a 64-bit build host cross-compiling for this
project's 32-bit i686 container target, that mismatch makes bindgen
parse libc headers as 64-bit while rustc itself correctly builds the
struct as 32-bit, so the generated assertion computes e.g.
`124_usize - 216_usize` and overflows at compile time
(error[E0080] in rbus-sys's generated bindings.rs).

Cargo always provides both HOST and TARGET to build scripts - use the
one that actually means "the architecture we're compiling for".

---
 rbus-sys/build.rs | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/rbus-sys/build.rs b/rbus-sys/build.rs
--- a/rbus-sys/build.rs
+++ b/rbus-sys/build.rs
@@ -9,7 +9,7 @@
 /// https://github.com/messense/homebrew-macos-cross-toolchains
 ///
 fn main() -> anyhow::Result<()> {
-    let host_triple = std::env::var("HOST")?;
+    let host_triple = std::env::var("TARGET")?;

     let base_dir = Path::new(RBUS_DIR).canonicalize();
     let base_dir = base_dir.map_err(|e| anyhow!("path {RBUS_DIR} not found: {e:?}"))?;
